  <h2>What a construction schedule does for a subcontractor</h2>
  <p>
    The general contractor has a master schedule. Yours is different: it is the sequence of your own
    crews through your own scope, at a level of detail the GC’s schedule never shows — which crew is
    on Level 2 rough-in the week the crane sets the rooftop units, whether controls rough-in can
    start before the submittal comes back, when the insulator needs to be on site. It is also the
    document that proves a delay was not yours. A schedule updated every week, with a status date
    and a % done on every task, is the record you reach for when the GC asks why the ceilings closed
    late.
  </p>
  <p>
    This template is built for that use. It is a spreadsheet, not scheduling software, and it stays
    honest by keeping the math visible: durations are workdays, end dates are computed, and the
    Gantt bars are drawn by formulas you can read.
  </p>

  <h2>How the Excel Gantt works</h2>
  <p>
    Every timeline column has a date in its header: the chart start date plus a multiple of the days
    per column. Every task row has a start date and an end date computed as
    <code>WORKDAY(start, duration − 1)</code>. A conditional-formatting rule on the grid then asks
    one question per cell: does this task overlap the dates this column covers? Written out, the
    rule is
    <code>start ≤ column date + days per column − 1</code> and <code>end ≥ column date</code>. If
    both are true the cell shades light blue.
  </p>
  <p>
    A second rule shades the done portion dark. It computes the point in the task that % done
    corresponds to — start plus the task length times % done — and shades any column that begins
    before that point. A third rule tints the column containing the status date, so the printed
    sheet shows where “today” falls against every bar. None of this needs a macro, and all of it
    works in Google Sheets and Numbers as well as Excel.
  </p>
  <p>
    The sequence check uses the predecessor column the same way. For each task with a predecessor
    number, it looks up that task’s end date and turns the start date red if the task begins on or
    before it. It is not a full critical-path calculation — the spreadsheet will not tell you which
    chain of tasks controls the finish date — but it catches the sequence errors that make a
    schedule physically impossible before you print it.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked example: six months of mechanical work</h2>
  <p>
    The completed example that ships with the download is a mechanical subcontractor’s schedule on a
    medical office building: 23 tasks across eleven phases, from mobilization on June 1, 2026 to
    closeout on November 18, with the status date set to August 31, the end of the period billed on
    the third pay application. The chart is set to 14 days per column so the whole job fits in the
    16 columns; switch it to 7 and the same rows become a 16-week weekly view.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read across the bars and the state of the job is visible without a meeting. Underground, both
    rough-ins and the crane day for RTU-1 to RTU-4 are complete and shaded dark. Duct mains on Level
    2 (25 workdays from August 10) are 65% done, hydronic piping to AHU-3 is 55%, and both bars
    change from dark to light in the column holding the status date. Controls rough-in, insulation,
    fixtures and testing are still light bars in September and October, and the summary row reports
    the whole scope at 55% complete, weighted by duration. The predecessor column carries the logic:
    controls rough-in (task 16) waits on the Level 2 ductwork (task 12), commissioning waits on
    testing and balancing, and the RTU curb task references change order CO-001, the relocation of
    the RTU-2 curb that added two days to the contract.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the level of detail that pays off. A schedule with a task called “HVAC” from June to
    November tells the GC nothing; one that shows the crane day on August 3 depending on curbs
    finished July 10 lets the superintendent plan the <a href="/solutions/roofing-contractors/"
      >roofer</a
    >, the electrician and the crane pad around you.
  </p>

  <h2>Schedule, look-ahead and Gantt software</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The baseline schedule</strong> is what this template holds: the whole scope, phase by phase,
      with the dates you committed to. Issue it once after the
      <a href="/templates/notice-to-proceed/">notice to proceed</a>, then revise it when change
      orders move dates.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A look-ahead</strong> is the next two or three weeks pulled out of the baseline and broken
      into crew-level detail for the weekly coordination meeting. Set the chart start to next Monday and
      7 days per column and this sheet becomes one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Gantt software</strong> earns its keep when tasks depend on each other across phases and
      one slip should move everything downstream. The spreadsheet flags the conflict; software reschedules
      it. Read about <a href="/features/project-management/"
        >dependencies and the critical path in BuildWorkPro</a
      > when you get there.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Common scheduling mistakes this template is built to catch</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Durations in calendar days on a five-day crew — a 15-day task “finishes” on a Sunday and the
      real end date is a week later.
    </li>
    <li>
      No predecessors — every task is scheduled to the earliest date someone hoped for, and the
      sequence falls apart the first week.
    </li>
    <li>
      Long bars — a 40-day task at 50% tells you nothing about whether it is on track. Split it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Updating irregularly — a schedule updated when someone asks is ignored; one updated every
      Monday is used.
    </li>
    <li>
      Compressing after a delay — pulling every downstream task in to hold the finish date, without
      adding crew or hours, is a schedule that will slip again next month.
    </li>
    <li>
      Losing the change order trail — added days that are not tied to a CO number cannot be defended
      later. Note the number on the task.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Delays belong in the <a href="/templates/daily-report/">daily report</a> the day they happen — the
    schedule shows the effect, the daily report proves the cause; our guide to
    <a href="/blog/construction-daily-report-template/">writing a daily report that holds up</a>
    covers what to record. As the job closes, the last tasks on this sheet hand off to the <a
      href="/templates/punch-list/">punch list</a
    >, and the same dates feed the cost-to-complete on your
    <a href="/templates/construction-budget/">construction budget</a>.
  </p>
